  ["#appraisal", "Free Expert Appraisal"],
  ["#about-joe", "About Joe"],
];

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "What Year Is My Fender Mustang?",
    a: "Start at the headstock and the bridge together. A small headstock with a gold transition logo is 1964 or 1965. A large headstock still wearing that gold logo is late 1965 to 1967. A large headstock with the bold black logo is 1967 or 1968 onward. Then read the vibrato plate: Pat. Pend. is roughly 1964 to 1966 and a patent number is 1967 and later. Those two checks together usually get you inside a couple of years, and the neck heel date and the pot codes close it from there.",
  },
  {
    q: "Why Doesn't My Mustang Have A Slab Fingerboard?",
    a: "Because it never should. Fender moved from the flat slab board to the curved veneer board in the middle of 1962, and the Mustang did not arrive until 1964, so a slab board was never a factory configuration on this model. Every vintage Mustang has the veneer board, which is curved on its underside where it meets the maple. If a Mustang in front of you genuinely has a slab board, the likeliest explanation is that the neck came from a different guitar.",
  },
  {
    q: "Is A Painted Headstock On A Competition Mustang Original?",
    a: "It can be, and on an early one it should be. The matching painted headstock ran from the introduction of the Competition finish into about 1969 or 1970, after which a natural, unpainted maple face became standard. So a painted headstock points at the early Competition years and a natural one points later. People often assume the painted headstock is the later, fancier option and it runs the other way, which is also why a 1969 with its original painted headstock sits above a later natural-headstock guitar on value.",
  },
  {
    q: "What Is A Competition Mustang Worth Compared To A Standard One?",
    a: "Quite a lot more, and the finish is the reason. In honest all-original condition a Competition guitar from 1969 to 1973 runs roughly $4,500 to $9,000, against roughly $2,200 to $3,800 for a CBS-era standard colour and $2,800 to $5,000 for a pre-CBS 1964 or 1965. Competition Orange is the rarest of the three colours and can run above that, and Competition Blue carries extra weight from the Nirvana association. Because the buyer is paying for the finish, a refinished Competition guitar loses most of the premium, which is a bigger gap than a refinish costs on a plain Mustang.",
  },
  {
    q: "Did Fender Make A Competition Burgundy Mustang?",
    a: "Not really. Fender's own catalogue listed the blue Competition finish as Competition Burgundy, but the guitars themselves are blue rather than burgundy. Treat it as a catalogue label attached to the blue one, not as a fourth colour to hunt for. The three Competition finishes are red, blue and orange.",
  },
  {
    q: "My Mustang Sounds Thin And Hollow On One Setting. Is Something Broken?",
    a: "Probably not. Each of the two slide switches has an on position, an off position in the middle, and a second on position that flips that pickup's phase. Throw the two switches in opposite directions and the pickups run out of phase with each other, which produces a thin, nasal honk by design. Put both switches the same way for the full sound, or centre one of them to run a single pickup. What is not stock is a series or humbucking mode, so a Mustang that does that has been rewired.",
  },
  {
    q: "How Do I Tell A Vintage Mustang From A Japanese Reissue?",
    a: "Look for a country-of-origin marking first. Fender's Japanese factory reissued the Mustang from around 1990, modelled on the 1969 Competition look, and those carry Made in Japan or Crafted in Japan on the neck or the serial, along with a modern serial format and modern pots. A vintage American guitar has a plain stamped serial on an F neck plate or an L-series plate and no country marking. The reissues were also built in 24 inch scale only, so a 22.5 inch short scale points at a vintage guitar. The price gap between the two is large enough to be worth the two minutes.",
  },
  {
    q: "Should I Take My Mustang Apart To Date It?",
    a: "Only as far as you need to. Everything in the first two passes reads with the guitar assembled, which is deliberate. The neck heel date and the pot codes are worth going after if the outside checks leave you between two years. Two cautions on this model: the vibrato is under spring tension, so do not unbolt it casually, and sixty-year-old celluloid guards crack at the screw ears. Send me photos before you reach for a screwdriver.",
  },
];

// ---- Schema ----
const articleSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Article",
  headline: "How To Date A Fender Mustang, Year By Year",
  description:
    "Date a vintage Fender Mustang from what you can see: headstock size, logo colour, the Dynamic Vibrato engraving, the pickguard pairing and the Competition finishes, 1964 to 1982.",
  author: { "@id": `${SITE_URL}/about-me/#person` },
  publisher: { "@id": `${SITE_URL}/` },
  image: `${SITE_URL}${assetSrc(OG)}`,
  datePublished: "2026-08-11",
  dateModified: "2026-08-11",
  inLanguage: "en-US",
  mainEntityOfPage: `${SITE_URL}${PAGE_PATH}`,
  isPartOf: { "@type": "WebPage", "@id": `${SITE_URL}${HUB_PATH}` },
};
